\title{Stars at Every Rational: The Berggren Tree of Pythagorean Triples
in the Poincar\'e Half-Plane}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Plot the Berggren ternary tree of primitive Pythagorean triples in the Poincar\'e
upper half-plane $\HH$ through the Euclid embedding $z(m,n)=(n+i)/m$, and the result
is a \emph{star map}: pencils of straight rays radiate from the ideal points $0$ and
$1$ and, less conspicuously, from $1/2$, $1/3$, $1/5$ and from every other visible
rational on the boundary. We explain the phenomenon completely by means of a single
integral linear form, the \emph{charge} $\chg{p/q}(m,n)=pm-qn$ of a Euclid seed at a
rational ideal point $p/q$ in lowest terms. We prove: that the nodes of a fixed
charge lie on one Euclidean ray from $p/q$, which is a hypercycle at hyperbolic
distance $\arsinh(|\chi|/q)$ from the geodesic over $p/q$; that the fan at $p/q$
realises every integer charge when $p+q$ is odd and exactly the odd charges when
$p+q$ is even, the charge-zero axis carrying the single node $(q,p)$ in the first
case and none in the second; that every admissible ray is infinite, and that on a
ray of odd charge $k$ at an odd/odd rational every window of $2|k|$ consecutive
parameters of a unimodular parametrisation carries exactly $2\varphi(|k|)$ nodes,
so the ray has arithmetic density $\varphi(|k|)/|k|$; that the charge measures
Diophantine approximation quality, the innermost ray consisting of the Farey
neighbours of $p/q$, and that every node lies on the innermost ray of two distinct
stars of smaller denominator; that adjacent rays of the star at $p/q$ are $y/q$
apart at plot height $y$, so the resolvable star centres at resolution
$\varepsilon$ are the Farey fractions of level $\lfloor y/\varepsilon\rfloor$,
counted by $\sum_{q\le Q}\varphi(q)$; and that the three tree moves act linearly
on the star parameter $(p,q)$, transporting fans while preserving charges,
primitivity, and the parity of $p+q$ --- so that the all-charge $0$-star and the
odd-charge $1$-star lie in permanently different transport classes, while the star
at $k/(k+1)$ is carried onto the $0$-star by the word $B_1^{\,k}$.
\end{abstract}

